            <ArticleTitle>Neointimal tissue healing patterns after paclitaxel-eluting balloon treatment of in-stent restenosis: optical coherence tomography and intravascular ultrasound insights.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E215-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An 80-year-old patient presented with severe in-stent restenosis of an everolimus-eluting stent implanted in the left anterior descending coronary artery 3 years previously. We obtained good angiographic result after paclitaxel-eluting balloon dilation. However, on optical coherence tomography (OCT), multiple, angiographically silent, in-stent, and edge-related dissections were readily recognized. Intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) revealed residual neointima with minor disruptions. At 9-month follow-up, an excellent angiographic result was demonstrated with complete resolution of the stent-related dissections on OCT. IVUS and OCT confirmed complete neointimal healing with a larger lumen. This case illustrates the value of OCT and IVUS to provide unique insights on the pathophysiological mechanisms and healing patterns of paclitaxel-eluting balloon treatment of in-stent restenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
